flow\" src=\"../assets/diagrams/connection-flow.svg\" />"]},{"s":36389,"e":36479,"k":"block","t":"Quick ConnectKTPH000","f":["<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#quick-connect\" title=\"Permanent link\">&para;</a>"]},{"s":36488,"e":36807,"k":"block","t":"Open with KTPH002KTPH000KTPH003+KTPH004KTPH001KTPH005 (or KTPH006Connections → Quick Connect…KTPH007). Enter host, port, username and authentication, and connect without saving. Frequently used connections appear as quick buttons; a live search filters them.","f":["<kbd class=\"key-control\">Ctrl</kbd>","<kbd class=\"key-k\">K</kbd>","<span class=\"keys\">","<span>","</span>","</span>","<strong>","</strong>"]},{"s":36840,"e":36940,"k":"block","t":"Connection ManagerKTPH000","f":["<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#connection-manager\" title=\"Permanent link\">&para;</a>"]},{"s":36949,"e":37144,"k":"block","t":"KTPH000Connections → Manage Connections…KTPH001 opens a searchable tree of saved connections (optionally grouped). From here you create, edit, duplicate, delete, import and export connections.","f":["<strong>","</strong>"]},{"s":37188,"e":37312,"k":"block","t":"Creating / editing a connectionKTPH000","f":["<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#creating-editing-a-connection\" title=\"Permanent link\">&para;</a>"]},{"s":37321,"e":37358,"k":"block","t":"The connection editor has these tabs:","f":[]},{"s":37388,"e":37391,"k":"block","t":"Tab","f":[]},{"s":37401,"e":37409,"k":"block","t":"Contents","f":[]},{"s":37447,"e":37457,"k":"block","t":"Connection","f":[]},{"s":37467,"e":37779,"k":"block","t":"Host, port, username, protocol (SSH / Mosh / Local Shell), authentication (password / key / keyboard-interactive), KTPH000Host key verificationKTPH001 (use default / verify / don't verify). For KTPH002Local ShellKTPH003 connections host, port, username and authentication are not required and are disabled.","f":["<strong>","</strong>","<strong>","</strong>"]},{"s":37800,"e":37817,"k":"block","t":"Terminal Settings","f":[]},{"s":37827,"e":37896,"k":"block","t":"Per-connection colors, font, ANSI/TrueColor handling, terminal effect","f":[]},{"s":37917,"e":37928,"k":"block","t":"SSH Tunnels","f":[]},{"s":37938,"e":37978,"k":"block","t":"Local / remote / dynamic port forwarding","f":[]},{"s":37999,"e":38010,"k":"block","t":"Jump Server","f":[]},{"s":38020,"e":38041,"k":"block","t":"Bastion-host chaining","f":[]},{"s":38062,"e":38078,"k":"block","t":"Terminal Logging","f":[]},{"s":38088,"e":38129,"k":"block","t":"Per-connection log format and destination","f":[]},{"s":38150,"e":38165,"k":"block","t":"Window Geometry","f":[]},{"s":38175,"e":38214,"k":"block","t":"Saved size/position for this connection","f":[]},{"s":38263,"e":38345,"k":"block","t":"ProtocolsKTPH000","f":["<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#protocols\" title=\"Permanent link\">&para;</a>"]},{"s":38653,"e":38656,"k":"block","t":"SSH","f":[]},{"s":38690,"e":38694,"k":"block","t":"Mosh","f":[]},{"s":38728,"e":38739,"k":"block","t":"Local Shell","f":[]},{"s":38813,"e":38962,"k":"block","t":"Standard SSH via Apache MINA SSHD. Supports password, public-key and keyboard-interactive authentication, keep-alive, and OSC 8 clickable hyperlinks.","f":[]},{"s":39004,"e":39140,"k":"block","t":"Roaming, latency-friendly Mosh transport (mosh4j). The Mosh backend is bundled in native builds; existing connections need no migration.","f":[]},{"s":39182,"e":39405,"k":"block","t":"Opens the KTPH000local machine'sKTPH001 shell in a terminal tab (no network) via a pty4j-backed pseudo-terminal. Host, port, username and authentication are not required. See KTPH002Local ShellKTPH003 below.","f":["<strong>","</strong>","<a href=\"#local-shell\">","</a>"]},{"s":39466,"e":39580,"k":"block","t":"SSH host-key verificationKTPH000","f":["<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#ssh-host-key-verification\" title=\"Permanent link\">&para;</a>"]},{"s":39589,"e":39854,"k":"block","t":"Interactive Terminal and SFTP connections use the same trust-on-first-use (TOFU) host-key store. Mosh uses it for the SSH bootstrap as well. Trust is keyed by the normalized host name and port, so different saved connections to the same endpoint share one decision.","f":[]},{"s":39862,"e":40363,"k":"block","t":"On the first connection, korTTY shows the key algorithm and OpenSSH SHA-256 fingerprint. Verify that fingerprint with the server administrator before selecting KTPH000YesKTPH001; KTPH002NoKTPH003 is the safe default. A matching key is accepted silently on later connections. If the server presents a different key, korTTY hard-blocks the connection, shows the expected and offered fingerprints, and does not retry because repeating the attempt cannot resolve a possible man-in-the-middle attack.","f":["<strong>","</strong>","<strong>","</strong>"]},{"s":40371,"e":41020,"k":"block","t":"The first-use prompt can be turned off for hosts where it is not wanted — set KTPH000Host key verificationKTPH001 on the connection editor's KTPH002ConnectionKTPH003 tab or in Quick Connect (KTPH004Use defaultKTPH005 / KTPH006VerifyKTPH007 / KTPH008Don't verifyKTPH009), per group via the Connection Manager's group context menu, or globally under KTPH010Settings → TerminalKTPH011. The relaxation is accept-new only: an unknown key is pinned without a prompt, but a key that differs from one already pinned for that host is still hard-blocked. See KTPH012Relaxing host-key verificationKTPH013.","f":["<strong>","</strong>","<em>","</em>","<strong>","</strong>","<strong>","</strong>","<strong>","</strong>","<strong>","</strong>","<a href=\"security.html#relaxing-host-key-verification\">","</a>"]},{"s":41028,"e":41344,"k":"block","t":"The interactive pins are stored atomically in KTPH000, with cross-process locking so two korTTY windows cannot overwrite each other's decisions. These endpoint-based pins are separate from the connection-ID-based pins used by unattended JobScheduler SSH, SFTP, and Rsync jobs.","f":["<code>~/.kortty/ssh-host-keys.properties</code>"]},{"s":41352,"e":41604,"k":"block","t":"When a new split connection is opened, the SSH handshake runs on a worker while a progress dialog keeps the JavaFX interface responsive. This allows both host-key confirmation and keyboard-interactive authentication to complete without blocking the UI.","f":[]},{"s":41630,"e":41716,"k":"block","t":"Local ShellKTPH000","f":["<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#local-shell\" title=\"Permanent link\">&para;</a>"]},{"s":41725,"e":42111,"k":"block","t":"A KTPH000Local ShellKTPH001 connection spawns a local pseudo-terminal (PTY) on your own machine instead of connecting to a remote host. It is selectable in both KTPH002Quick ConnectKTPH003 and the KTPH004Connection ManagerKTPH005; for these connections host, port, username and authentication are not required (and are disabled in the dialogs), and no password prompt is shown.","f":["<strong>","</strong>","<strong>","</strong>","<strong>","</strong>"]},{"s":42142,"e":42238,"k":"block","t":"Choosing a shellKTPH000","f":["<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#choosing-a-shell\" title=\"Permanent link\">&para;</a>"]},{"s":42269,"e":42277,"k":"block","t":"Platform","f":[]},{"s":42287,"e":42294,"k":"block","t":"Options","f":[]},{"s":42332,"e":42339,"k":"block","t":"Windows","f":[]},{"s":42349,"e":42738,"k":"block","t":"KTPH002PowerShellKTPH003 (default) or KTPH004cmd.exeKTPH005. KTPH006Git BashKTPH007, KTPH008CygwinKTPH009 and KTPH010WSLKTPH011 are also offered as presets — but only when actually installed (Git Bash/Cygwin are detected via their usual install locations / KTPH000; WSL appears only when KTPH001 is present and at least one distribution is installed).","f":["<code>PATH</code>","<code>wsl.exe</code>","<strong>","</strong>","<strong>","</strong>","<strong>","</strong>","<strong>","</strong>","<strong>","</strong>"]},{"s":42759,"e":42772,"k":"block","t":"macOS / Linux","f":[]},{"s":42782,"e":42885,"k":"block","t":"Defaults to your KTPH000 (falling back to KTPH001 or KTPH002).","f":["<code>$SHELL</code>","<code>/bin/zsh</code>","<code>/bin/bash</code>"]},{"s":42918,"e":43289,"k":"block","t":"A free-form KTPH003Custom commandKTPH004 field accepts any executable with arguments (e.g. KTPH000, KTPH001, a Git Bash path), and an optional KTPH005start directoryKTPH006 can be set. The command parser is quote-aware, so shell paths containing spaces — like KTPH002 — launch correctly.","f":["<code>pwsh.exe</code>","<code>wsl.exe -d Ubuntu</code>","<code>\"C:\\Program Files\\Git\\bin\\bash.exe\"</code>","<strong>","</strong>","<strong>","</strong>"]},{"s":43337,"e":43467,"k":"block","t":"Terminal features in local shellsKTPH000","f":["<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#terminal-features-in-local-shells\" title=\"Permanent link\">&para;</a>"]},{"s":43476,"e":44151,"k":"block","t":"Terminal logging and recording, plus the AI input/data hooks, work for local shells via a shared KTPH000 interface. Typed and pasted agent requests use the same byte-level input path, and terminal file actions plus local agent runs follow the interactive shell's current directory. macOS/Linux use the local process directory; native PowerShell and cmd use absolute prompt paths. WSL, Git Bash, Cygwin, and custom commands are best-effort when their shell path namespace differs from the host filesystem, and an unmappable directory produces an explicit error instead of a wrong-file fallback. Features that depend on an SSH channel stay SSH-only.","f":["<code>ObservableTtyConnector</code>"]},{"s":44214,"e":44238,"k":"block","t":"AI Agent in local shells","f":[]},{"s":44246,"e":44438,"k":"block","t":"The KTPH000AI AgentKTPH001 and KTPH002AI PlanningKTPH003 also run in local shells on Windows, macOS and Linux — see KTPH004AI assistantKTPH005.","f":["<strong>","</strong>","<strong>","</strong>","<a href=\"ai-assistant.html#ai-agent-and-ai-planning\">","</a>"]},{"s":44480,"e":44590,"k":"block","t":"Tunnels KTPH001 jump serversKTPH000","f":["<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#tunnels-jump-servers\" title=\"Permanent link\">&para;</a>","&amp;"]},{"s":44605,"e":44812,"k":"block","t":"KTPH003SSH tunnelsKTPH004 — forward ports through the connection: KTPH005localKTPH006 (KTPH000), KTPH007remoteKTPH008 (KTPH001) or KTPH009dynamic / SOCKSKTPH010 (KTPH002).","f":["<code>-L</code>","<code>-R</code>","<code>-D</code>","<strong>","</strong>","<strong>","</strong>","<strong>","</strong>","<strong>","</strong>"]},{"s":44822,"e":45013,"k":"block","t":"KTPH000Jump server (bastion)KTPH001 — route the connection through an intermediate host; SSH terminal and SFTP sessions both hop through it. See KTPH002Jump ServerKTPH003.","f":["<strong>","</strong>","<a href=\"jump-server.html\">","</a>"]},{"s":45028,"e":45104,"k":"block","t":"KTPH000","f":["<img alt=\"Jump server flow\" src=\"../assets/diagrams/jump-server-flow.svg\" />"]},{"s":45144,"e":45258,"k":"block","t":"Import from other clientsKTPH000","f":["<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#import-from-other-clients\" title=\"Permanent link\">&para;</a>"]},{"s":45267,"e":45479,"k":"block","t":"KTPH000Connections → Import…KTPH001 reads connection files from KTPH002MTPuTTYKTPH003, KTPH004MobaXtermKTPH005 and KTPH006PuTTY Connection ManagerKTPH007, with group filtering and credential handling.","f":["<strong>","</strong>","<strong>","</strong>","<strong>","</strong>","<strong>","</strong>"]},{"s":45542,"e":45554,"k":"block","t":"More to come","f":[]},{"s":45562,"e":45777,"k":"block","t":"This page is part of the scaffolded guide. The full feature library — SFTP, snippets, JobScheduler, AI assistant KTPH000 tools, terminal recording, security, and the complete settings tables — is being filled in next.","f":["&amp;"]},{"s":46655,"e":46670,"k":"text","t":"\n  Back to top\n","f":[]},{"s":46913,"e":46954,"k":"text","t":"MIT-licensed · Developed by Daniel Mengel","f":[]},{"s":47000,"e":47012,"k":"text","t":"\n    korTTY ","f":[]},{"s":47020,"e":47026,"k":"text","t":"v2.7.0","f":[]}]}